# Sacred-Monument Geometry vs Crop-Circle Families — Round 1 Consultation
*Historian of architecture & geometry; all ratios verified numerically; fringe claims flagged.*

---

## 1. Documented geometric systems, monument by monument

### 1.1 Gothic cathedrals (ad quadratum / ad triangulum, vesica piscis)
- **Documented practice.** Medieval master masons transmitted proportioning through "constructive geometry": **ad quadratum** (successive squares, ratio 1 : √2 ≈ 1.4142) and **ad triangulum** (successive equilateral triangles, ratio 1 : √3 ≈ 1.7321). Primary evidence: the portfolio of **Villard de Honnecourt** (c. 1230s), the **Roriczer** family booklets (*Büchlein von der Fialen Gerechtigkeit*, 1486 — a pinnace generated by successive squares inscribed via circle/quadrature), the German lodge statutes, and the surviving **tracé (full-scale floor drawings)** at cathedrals such as Lübeck, Regensburg, Milan.
- **Chartres specifically.** The west façade, elevation proportions, and crossing have been shown to derive from square-based grids (ad quadratum and "square-root" progression). The three rose windows are 12-fold (West rose) and 12-fold (North and South roses). Chartres's nave elevation (c. 32 m) follows a triadic scheme of arcade / triforium / clerestory; claims that the whole cathedral is governed by the vesica piscis are **partly supported, partly speculative** — the vesica (√3) appears in bay proportions, but sweeping "everything is a vesica" claims (e.g., in popular sacred-geometry literature) exceed the evidence.
- **Key scholarship:** Paul Frankl, "The Secret of the Medieval Masons" (*Art Bulletin*, 1945); Shelby, "The Geometrical Knowledge of Mediaeval Master Masons" (*Speculum*, 1977); Robert Bork, *The Geometry of Creation* (2011); Marie-Thérèse Zenner ed., *Villard's Legacy* (2004); on Chartres, Otto von Simson, *The Gothic Cathedral* (1956) — note von Simson's light-theology φ readings are contested.
- **Symmetry order:** plans and façades are strongly **2- and 4-fold** (square grids, crossing, quincunx-like partis); windows introduce **6-, 8-, 12-fold** radial orders (rosettes).

### 1.2 Hindu temples: vastu-purusha mandala
- **Documented practice.** The **vastu-purusha mandala** is a square divination/planning grid overlaid on the building site, personified as the cosmic man. Canonical forms include the **Manduka mandala (64 padas, 8×8)** and the **Paramashayika / Paramasayika mandala (81 padas, 9×9)**; śāstras (Mayamata, Mānasāra, Śilpa-prakāśa) assign deities to each pada and prescribe placement of garbhagriha (sanctum) on the central Brahma pada(s).
- **Symmetry order:** **4-fold square grid with 8×8 or 9×9 subdivision**; the mandala is concentric (padas → squares around the center) with **four cardinal gates** — this is the direct architectural ancestor of the "mandala = concentric circles + 4 gates" template.
- **Key scholarship:** Stella Kramrisch, *The Hindu Temple* (1946); Adam Hardy, *Indian Temple Architecture: Form and Transformation* (1995); Michael Meister on the *latina* nāgara geometry; Bruno Dagens, *Mayamata* (translation, 1976).

### 1.3 Angkor Wat
- **Documented geometry.** Quincunx of five towers = Mount Meru; a moated rectangle ~**1.5 km × 1.3 km**; temple enclosure about 1025 m × 800 m; the plan is governed by a 4-fold cruciform scheme with processional axes.
- **Astronomy (peer-reviewed claim, not fringe).** **Stencel, Gifford & Moravetz, "Astronomy and Cosmology at Angkor Wat" (*Science*, 1976)** documented solar alignments: at the **summer solstice** the Sun rises directly over the central tower for an observer at the western causeway, and they identified day-count correspondences between axial dimensions (in Khmer cubits) and calendrical numbers (e.g., 360 / 354 / 366 and multiples). These are measurement-based claims, broadly accepted as intentional alignment; stronger "phi in Angkor" claims circulating online are **not supported**.
- **Symmetry order:** 4-fold plan, 5-element quincunx, 3-level vertical hierarchy.

### 1.4 Borobudur (c. 800, Central Java)
- **Documented structure.** Base square ≈ **123 m**; **nine platforms**: **six square terraces + three circular terraces**; vertical reading as a **threefold Buddhist cosmogram — Kāmadhātu (world of desire, the buried foot), Rūpadhātu (world of forms, square galleries with reliefs), Arūpadhātu (formless world, circular terraces with 72 perforated stupas around a central stupa)**; ~2,672 relief panels, 504 Buddha statues. The "Borobudur as mandala" reading (Walker's) is standard textbook interpretation, though some scholars (e.g., Miksic, Soekmono) caution it is a **stupa/mountain (Meru) symbol** more than a rigorous two-dimensional mandala diagram; both readings coexist in the literature.
- **Symmetry order:** **4-fold square** terraces + **3 circular levels**; staircase axes on all four sides; perforation counts on stupas use 24/32/48 rhombic openings (arithmetically interesting: these are 8×3, 8×4, 8×6 — multiples of 8, i.e., 4-fold × 2).
- **Scholarship:** J. G. de Casparis, P. Mus (*Barabudur*, 1935), John Miksic, *Borobudur: Golden Tales of the Buddhas* (1990); Soekmono, *Chandi Borobudur* (1976).

### 1.5 Greek temples — the Parthenon (447–432 BCE)
- **Documented optical corrections** (these are solid, measured facts): **entasis** (convex column swelling), **curvature of the stylobate and entablature** (rising ~6 cm at the ends / ~11 cm on the flanks above a straight line), **inclination of columns** (~7 cm inward over their height, axes meeting ~2.4 km up), **corner contraction** and **thickened corner columns**. Sources: Penrose, *An Investigation of the Principles of Athenian Architecture* (1851/1888); Balanos's restorations; M. Korres, *From Pentelicon to the Parthenon* (1995).
- **Documented proportion:** the Parthenon is governed by the ratio **4 : 9** — width to length of stylobate (30.88 × 69.5 m ≈ 1 : 2.251), 8 × 17 columns (= 2n+1 relation), height of order to width ≈ 4:9. This is the classic **integer-ratio canon**, not φ.
- **φ claims:** asserted by some (e.g., van der Laan's followers, and popular literature) that the architrave height to width or the façade fits a golden rectangle — **Markowsky (1992) shows the measurements don't fit**; there is **no textual evidence** Greeks used φ in architecture (Euclid's definition is 4th century BCE, later than the Parthenon, and concerns mathematics, not building). See §4.

### 1.6 Giza
- **Documented: the seked system.** Egyptian slope was expressed as a **seked** — horizontal run in palms per 1-cubit (7-palm) rise. Rhind Mathematical Papyrus problems 56–59 work seked calculations. **Khufu's Great Pyramid: seked 5 palms 2 digits = 5½ palms → rise:run = 14:11, slope angle ≈ 51°50′ (51.84°)**, confirmed by Petrie's survey (mean 51°50′40″). Khafre: 3:2 (56.3°); Menkaure ~ 51°20′; Bent Pyramid: lower 54°27′ (7:5), upper ~43°22′. So **slope was an integer rational choice, period-documented.**
- **π "coincidence":** half-perimeter / height = 2×440/280 = **22/7 = 3.142857** (π to 0.04%). This is **arithmetically inevitable given a 14:11 slope** — any pyramid built with seked 5½ automatically yields 22/7. Whether Egyptians noticed is unknowable; no Egyptian text states a circle-perimeter/pyramid relation. Documented fact = seked; the π claim is a numerological reading of the same integers.
- **φ claim:** apothem/half-base = √(280²+220²)/220 = **1.6186 ≈ φ (0.03%)** — again a consequence of 14:11. **Herodotus' alleged statement (II.124) that the face area equals the height-squared is a misreading** (Herodotus says the side of each face's *base* is equal to the height... the text is ambiguous; the φ reading was popularized much later, notably by Charles Piazzi Smyth in the 19th century pyramidology movement — Smyth's work is the fountainhead of Giza numerology and was debunked in his own era by Petrie's accurate survey).
- **Scholarship:** W. M. F. Petrie, *The Pyramids and Temples of Gizeh* (1883); Corinna Rossi, *Architecture and Mathematics in Ancient Egypt* (Cambridge, 2004) — the definitive skeptical/archaeometrical treatment; Legon, "On Pyramid Dimensions and Proportions" (*DE*, 1991).

### 1.7 Mesoamerica — El Castillo (Chichén Itzá, c. 9th–12th c.)
- **Documented calendrical arithmetic.** **Four stairways × 91 steps + top platform = 365** (haab year). **Nine terraces, split by stairways into 18 panels on each face → 18 months of 20 days.** **52 panels per face on the terraces → the 52-year Calendar Round.** The equinox shadow of a descending serpent on the north balustrade (documented, though its intentionality is debated by archaeoastronomers). Base ≈ 55.5 m; height ≈ 30 m; slope ~45°.
- **Symmetry order:** **4-fold**, nested pyramid-on-pyramid (the 1930s excavations found an earlier structure inside with the famous red jaguar throne)